russia's war on ukraine is again  impacting the occupied crimea peninsula  russian officials say explosions at an  air base there have killed one person  and injured several others  videos shared on social media show  several large blasts at the novo  fedoreska facility  russia's defense ministry says the  explosions were detonations of stored  ammunition and not the result of any  attack  kiev denied any responsibility for the  incident  a senior ukrainian official suggested  the blasts may have been a work of  saboteurs inside crimea  russia illegally annexed crimea in 2014  an action that ukraine's president  vladimir zelinsky says was directly  connected to the current war  today there's a lot of attention on the  crimea issue  and rightly so because crimea is  ukrainian and we will never renounce it  we will not forget that it was the  occupation of crimea that was the  beginning of the russian war against  ukraine  dw correspondent matthias berlinga is  following the story from kiev i asked  him whether russia's claim that the  explosions were an accident seemed  plausible well yeah the ukrainian the  ministry of defense has said that it  advises the people in occupied crimea to  adhere to smoking regulations and russia  has said it was an accident but if you  look at the video footage that people  have shut from these explosions then  it's very clear that  an accidental fire is not very probable  two explosions happening at the same  time at two different locations that  pretty much looks like an attack um now  who did it and why and how um is another  question we have a several voices out of  the ukrainian government suggesting that  it might have either been a partisan  attack or some kind of a  rocket or uav attack um uh this is very  contradictive and that's probably  intended because uh ukraine wouldn't  want to give russia a clue about what  has been happening and wouldn't um uh  want russia to draw some consequences  in further cases um some security  regulations etc uh from this so um we  are at the moment we don't really know  what has happened but it's pretty clear  that this is a major strike on  russian uh military infrastructure there  that was it was a big aerodrome that was  used ukrainians say for attacks on  the ukrainian  inner territories and  we have also seen these were big  explosions a lot of ammunition several  planes destroyed so it's definitely a  military  success for ukraine and that's probably  also the reason why russia is not  admitting uh that it has been an attack  because it would make  it would make look russia pretty  vulnerable on crimea which is one of  their main  military  bases one of the most militarized places  in this region  as we all know matthias russia illegally  annexed crimea in 2014 and has occupied  it ever since if ukraine makes  sufficient gains in the current conflict  could we see ukraine trying to take back  crimea  if is the big question because at the  moment of course this looks pretty much  impossible whether the conditions will  change  in a way that uh uh ukraine might  attempt to take back crimea is of course  the big question is probably the last  one considering the last territory that  ukraine might try to take back  considering that it's pretty militarized  it's not as accessible as other  territories that russia is occupying  that was dw correspondent matthias  berlinga speaking to me a little while  here from kiev  and i'm joined now from london by  military analyst and conflict researcher  mike martin welcome to dw mike um can  you tell us who who do you think was  behind these explosions in crimea  of course it was the uh ukrainians um  we've heard this before from the  russians when the moskva sank your your  viewers will probably remember this  russian ship that sank there were all  sorts of stories  and then it turned out that actually the  ukrainians had fired a missile at it the  reason of course that the ukrainians are  being slightly coy about this they're  saying oh well you know maybe it was us  maybe it wasn't us it's because what  this does is it it spreads confusion and  fear amongst the russians and  particularly the russian forces based in  crimea and and just north of crimea and  her son and this is exactly what the  russians have been doing for the last 10  years so they're getting a taste of  their own medicine is there any  information available about the damage  that these explosions caused  uh  not with complete clarity what we do  know is that that base had a number of  um fairly advanced russian fighter  aircraft on them  prior to the explosion so we can assume  that at least and this this is sort of  eight to ten aircraft we can assume that  these have been damaged but what we do  also know is that there were some  explosions from either the partisan  attack or a missile strike or whatever  and then sometime later there was a much  larger series of explosions and what  that tells us is that  effectively the initial strike then set  off ammunition or fuel which is what  you'd expect to have on a base of that  size and so  the russians have lost quite a sizable  quantity and if you look at the pictures  sort of the mushroom cloud raising up  the russians have lost quite a sizable  quantity of fuel and ammunition  if this was a ukrainian attack does it  signify  a strategy a strategy shift from kiev  actually i think it's a continuation of  a strategy effectively what they're  doing by striking in crimea is pointing  out to the russians that nowhere on  occupied ukrainian territory is safe and  for the russians crimea is the most  important territory that they occupy  it's more important than donbass so if  the ukrainians can hit  the russians in crimea  what that will do is it will force the  russians to pull forces and defenses out  of other areas into crimea to defend it  and then that of course gives the  ukrainians more options about where they  want to attack next the momentum  really has shifted in this war it's now  the ukrainians that are dictating the  story whereas  previously it's been the russians that  have been forcing the ukrainians to  respond to them we've seen reports of  more russian shelling of central ukraine  overnight can you tell me tell us any  more about that  um  the shelling of civilian areas by  russia  is if you look at it in military terms  it doesn't achieve anything so when you  see the ukrainians striking russian  military targets and the russians  striking ukrainian civilian targets that  also tells us something it tells us that  russia is  feeling under pressure and feeling  somewhat powerless and it's striking out  at civilians because although attacks on  civilians obviously illegal and  obviously terrifying for those civilians  in strict military terms they don't  actually advance the russian cause at  all in fact they probably set it back  because as we've seen attacks on  civilians have galvanized the ukrainian  population  unified them to fight against the  russians  mike martin we'll have to leave it there  thanks so much for that really  fascinating analysis  thank you very much  you
 

